<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahabharata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE–400 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahabharata is one of the two great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epics of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, traditionally attributed to the sage Vyasa and composed over several centuries between roughly 400 BCE and 400 CE. Its central story is the war at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> between the Pandava and Kaurava branches of the Kuru dynasty. With about 100,000 verses it is among the longest poems ever written, and it contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="Mahabharata" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its court patronised literature in Bengali as well as Persian: Maladhar Basu composed the Sri Krishna Vijaya under Rukunuddin Barbak Shah, Krittibas Ojha produced the most popular Bengali Ramayana in this era, and the governor of Chittagong under Husain Shah sponsored a Bengali rendering of the <b>Mahabharata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Atharvaveda treats the Magadhas as outsiders to Vedic orthodoxy, and the <b>Mahabharata</b> preserves legends of an earlier Brihadratha dynasty and its king Jarasandha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa ordered the <b>Mahabharata</b> translated into Old Javanese in 996.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The great epics, the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the Ramayana, told the stories of gods and heroes; law books defined dharma, the duties of each class and stage of life; and devotion to personal gods, above all Vishnu and Shiva, grew alongside the older rites.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The two great epics, the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the Ramayana, took shape over many centuries, roughly between 400 BCE and 400 CE.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The 33 surviving poems of the Paripatal praise Vishnu, Durga and Murugan, and the Kalittokai mentions Krishna, Shiva, Murugan, the Pandava brothers of the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the goddess Ganga.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
